.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ccc
- the micronix C compiler
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ccc [ option ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ccc compiles C programs for micronix.  It drives the compiler passes,
c0, c1, c2 and peep, assembles with as, and links with ld, so a single
command turns source into a running program.  A file ending in .c is
compiled, one in .s is assembled, and .o and .a files are handed to
the linker.  With no -c or -s, the result is an executable called
a.out, or the name given with -o.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-o name	Name the output file rather than a.out.

.ti -10
-c	Compile and assemble, but do not link; leave the .o.

.ti -10
-s	Compile only; leave the assembly in a .s.

.ti -10
-k	Keep every intermediate file the passes produce.

.ti -10
-O	Run the peephole optimizer.  Not optional in practice: it is what makes the image small enough to leave the program any heap.

.ti -10
-S	Strip symbols from the output.

.ti -10
-I dir	Add dir to the include search path.

.ti -10
-i dir	Set the system include directory.

.ti -10
-D var	Define a preprocessor macro.

.ti -10
-E	Preprocess only; write the result to the standard output.

.ti -10
-l lib	Link with liblib.a.

.ti -10
-L dir	Add dir to the library search path.

.ti -10
-m system	Choose the target: micronix (the default) or cpm.

.ti -10
-x	Print each command as it is run.

.ti -10
-n	Print the commands without running them.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The compiler and its passes live beside ccc, in /libexec, and are
looked up from where ccc was found, so a copy of ccc built for CP/M
would look for its own passes rather than micronix's.
.sp
The -O flag is the difference between a program that fits a 64k image
and one that does not: every byte of code is a byte the program cannot
allocate for its data.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
